          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.74,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Quadrilaterals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": "The Carpenter’s Problem shows that rectangles can also be defined as follows—"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.13,
            0.848,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": "**Rectangle:** A rectangle is a quadrilateral whose diagonals are equal and bisect each other."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.171,
            0.848,
            0.229
          ],
          "content": "Observe how different this definition is from the earlier one. Yet, both capture the same class of quadrilaterals. Further, it turns out that the first definition can be simplified."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.238,
            0.848,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "？ In the earlier definition, we stated that a rectangle has (a) opposite sides of equal length, and (b) all angles equal to $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . Would we be wrong if we just define a rectangle as a quadrilateral in which all the angles are $9 0 ^ { \\circ } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.306,
            0.848,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": "？ If you think that this definition is incomplete, try constructing a quadrilateral in which the angles are all $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ but the opposite sides are not equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.367,
            0.611,
            0.407
          ],
          "content": "Are you able to construct such a quadrilateral? Let us prove why this is impossible."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.412,
            0.847,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": "**Deduction 4—What is the shape of a quadrilateral with all the** angles equal to $\\mathbf { 9 0 ^ { \\circ } ? }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.459,
            0.847,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "？ Consider a quadrilateral ABCD with all angles measuring $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . What can we say about the opposite sides of such a quadrilateral?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.503,
            0.811,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": "Join BD. ∆BAD and ∆DCB seem congruent. Can we justify this claim?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.531,
            0.483,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.489,
            0.579,
            0.848,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "Two equalities can be directly seen in the triangles. What can we say about $\\angle 1$ and ∠2?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.695,
            0.846,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "Recall that we tackled a very similar problem in Deduction 2. We can use the same reasoning here."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.735,
            0.483,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.889,
            0.344,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Since} \\angle \\mathrm{B} = 90^{\\circ},",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.908,
            0.336,
            0.926
          ],
          "content": "\\angle 3 + \\angle 1 = 90^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.502,
            0.731,
            0.838,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": "",
